# bodytracker

Full-body tracking for **VRChat** from an **Intel RealSense D415**, sent over OSC.
Works with VRChat on **Quest standalone** — no PC VR, no SteamVR, no SlimeVR
hardware. The machine running this becomes a tracking server on your LAN.

```
D415 ──► MediaPipe pose ──► depth lift ──► stabilise ──► predict ──► OSC ──► VRChat
        (2D landmarks)    (real metres)   (gate/bones/  (latency)  (1 packet
                                           smooth/fill)             per frame)
```

MediaPipe estimates a 3D pose from a single camera, but its depth axis is
*guessed* from limb proportions. This replaces that guess with **measured depth**
from the RealSense, then stabilises and latency-compensates the result.

---

## ⚠️ Status: works, but unverified inside VRChat

Read this before relying on it.

Everything up to the network has been measured on real hardware: 100% tracking,
4.7 mm joint jitter, 3.8 mm positional error at display time. What has **not**
been confirmed is the last step — that VRChat's FBT calibration maps our trackers
onto the right body parts and interprets our rotations as intended. Desktop
VRChat hides all FBT settings without a headset, so that test needs a Quest and
has not been run.

Two things could be wrong, and both are one-line fixes:

- **Tracker numbering.** We follow SlimeVR's convention (hip=1, feet=2/3,
  knees=4/5, chest=6, elbows=7/8). VRChat's own docs never state a mapping. If
  calibration puts trackers on the wrong limbs, edit `TRACKER_ROLES` in
  `transform.py`.
- **Rotations.** The maths is proven correct in isolation and the values are
  stable, but nothing has confirmed VRChat reads them as intended. Run with
  `--no-rotations` if limbs orient oddly.

## Requirements

- **Intel RealSense D415** (other D400 models likely work, untested)
- **Python 3.10–3.12** — 3.12 recommended; MediaPipe has no 3.13+ wheel
- **~2.5 m of clear floor**, so the camera sees you head to feet
- **A lit room** — see [Known limitations](#known-limitations)

## Install

### Windows

Every dependency is a wheel.

```powershell
py -3.12 -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\pip install -e ".[camera]"
.venv\Scripts\python bodytracker.py fetch
```

### Linux

```bash
python3.12 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -e .
.venv/bin/python bodytracker.py fetch
```

`pyrealsense2` is the catch: on recent distributions there is no apt package and
no wheel for Python 3.12, so it must be built from source. `SETUP.md` documents
the exact build — including two flags without which it fails — and the udev rule
the camera needs to be usable without root.

## Quick start

```bash
# 1. check your framing -- nothing is sent
python bodytracker.py preview

# 2. send to VRChat on the Quest, with the overlay window
python bodytracker.py run 192.168.1.42 --preview
```

Then in VRChat: **Options → OSC → Enabled**, set your real height in settings, and
**Quick Menu → Launch Pad → Calibrate FBT**.

If your camera is mounted **upside down**, add `--flip`.

| subcommand | what it does |
|---|---|
| `run [host]` | track and send OSC. `--preview` adds the overlay in the same process |
| `preview` | camera + overlay only, sends nothing |
| `fake [host]` | synthetic trackers, no camera — separates network problems from tracking ones |
| `listen` | watch VRChat's outgoing OSC on 9001, proving OSC is really enabled |
| `measure` | record once, replay through each stabilisation stage |
| `diagnose` | why joints get dropped, and whether depth or landmarks dominate the error |
| `fetch` | download the pose models |

## How well does it work

Measured on a real body, 542 frames:

| metric | value |
|---|---|
| tracking rate | 100% |
| joint jitter | 4.7 mm |
| positional error at display time | 3.8 mm |
| limb-length spread | 26.8 mm |
| framerate | 28–30 fps |

Stabilisation accounts for most of that: jitter −80%, worst glitch −75%, and
ankle availability 71% → 100% through occlusion filling.

## Known limitations

**Occlusion is the real one.** A single camera sees one side of you. Turn 90° and
your far leg hides behind the near one; turn 180° and it is guessing. IMU trackers
(SlimeVR) do not care which way you face — that is why they dominate the market.
If you dance or turn constantly this will frustrate you. For sitting, standing and
conversation it is fine.

**Lighting.** Landmarks come from the RGB image, so this does **not** work in the
dark despite being a depth camera. The failure is asymmetric and easy to
misdiagnose: the depth stream still looks perfect while tracking has stopped. In
`preview`, `NO POSE DETECTED` alongside a good depth image (press `d`) means *too
dark*, not *broken*.

**Repeated open/close degrades the camera.** After several start/stop cycles in
one session the stream can collapse to ~0.7 fps with no error at all. The app
detects this and tells you to replug. It is a hardware state, not a configuration
problem.

**Room depth.** You need roughly 2.5 m of clear floor. `preview` shows `HEAD in
frame` / `FEET in frame` — back up until both are green.

## Which trackers to send

Default is **hip + chest + both feet**. VRChat's docs warn that fewer trackers
often track better, but that is about *reliability*: its IK compensates well for a
**missing** point and badly for a **wrong** one. Measured per role over 446 frames:

| role | idx | present | jitter | |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| hip | 1 | 100% | 4.2 mm | core |
| left_foot | 2 | 99% | 7.9 mm | standard FBT |
| right_foot | 3 | 100% | 4.8 mm | standard FBT |
| left_knee | 4 | 100% | 6.7 mm | optional |
| right_knee | 5 | 100% | 5.6 mm | optional |
| chest | 6 | 100% | **3.1 mm** | core — our steadiest point |
| left_elbow | 7 | 100% | 5.5 mm | not recommended |
| right_elbow | 8 | 94% | 6.7 mm | not recommended |

**Elbows are not recommended**: your hands come from the Quest controllers, so an
elbow tracker constrains an arm whose endpoint is already authoritative, and a
slightly-wrong elbow fights the controller. Knees add little, because VRChat's IK
already infers them from hip and foot.

Indices follow **SlimeVR's mapping**, the de-facto standard (`VRCOSCHandler.kt`,
which carries the comment *"Don't change as third party applications may rely on
this"*).

## Options

```
bodytracker.py run [host] [--port 9000]
    --preview                       overlay window in the same process
    --roles hip,chest,left_foot,right_foot
    --flip                          camera mounted upside down
    --lead-ms 50                    latency compensation (0 disables)
    --model full                    lite | full | heavy
    --min-cutoff 0.5 --beta 0.35    smoothing
    --no-bones --no-gate --no-fill  disable a stabilisation stage
    --no-rotations --no-filter      disable rotations / depth post-processing
```

Every `--no-*` switch exists so a stage can be measured against its own absence
with `bodytracker.py measure`, not as a workaround.

## Why these defaults

Each was chosen by measurement, and several contradict the obvious guess:

- **MEDIUM_DENSITY** depth preset — ankle availability 96% vs 58% for
  HIGH_DENSITY. A *static-scene* benchmark says HIGH_ACCURACY is best; on an
  actual body it is the worst of the three. Benchmark on a person.
- **848×480 @ 30 fps** — 1280×720 carries 50% more depth noise at identical
  coverage.
- **`full` model** — `heavy` is 3× slower with a body in frame (15 fps), and
  halving the update rate costs more than its per-frame accuracy gains.
- **Hole filling `nearest`, not the SDK default `farthest`** — the default fills a
  gap on your torso with the wall behind you.
- **50 ms prediction lead** — cuts display-time error 9.0 → 3.8 mm, and the
  optimum lands on the independently measured pipeline latency.

`SETUP.md` has the full tables, plus the RealSense options deliberately *not* used
and why.

## Development

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest          # 35 tests, no camera required
ruff check .
```

The tests cover what broke silently during development: the Euler conversion, the
axis transform, the OSC wire format, and each stabilisation stage. Every one of
those bugs was invisible in the preview window, which is why they are tests rather
than something you eyeball.

